# Comprehensive Human Neuroanatomy Pathway & Fiber Atlas — Consolidated Reference

Scope: Human neuroanatomy pathways, fasciculi, projection systems, commissures, local fiber systems, neuromodulatory systems, and multisynaptic circuits.

[[!toc levels=5]]

Interactive viewer for this atlas: <https://diyhpl.us/~bryan/projects/neuroanatomy-viewer/>

For writing to these pathways rather than describing them, see [[circuit editing|circuit_editing]].

## Representation rules

1. **Entity type matters.** A named fasciculus, monosynaptic projection, diffuse neuromodulatory system, shared conduit, and multisynaptic loop are not interchangeable anatomical objects.
2. **Directionality is evidence-dependent.** Diffusion tractography can support course/topography but does not by itself establish synaptic direction.
3. **Laminar/cellular specificity is conservative.** A cortical layer, transmitter, or cell type is listed only when it is reasonably supported at the relevant pathway level.
4. **Oscillations are usually network properties.** Alpha/beta/theta/gamma labels are treated as task/state/network correlates unless the evidence isolates the pathway.
5. **Human evidence is prioritized.** Comparative tracing/electrophysiology can clarify mechanisms, but human versus animal evidence should not be silently conflated.
6. **Crossings and conduits are explicit.** Decussations, internal-capsule compartments, peduncles, and other landmarks are represented where clinically/anatomically important.
7. **Uncertainty stays visible.** Debated bundles and speculative mechanistic interpretations are explicitly marked.

## Query-use convention

When using this atlas, adhere to the following conventions:

- Report **source → relay(s)/course → destination**, then crossing/laterality and major neighboring landmarks.
- State whether the object is a **tract/fasciculus, projection system, local fiber system, diffuse modulatory system, shared conduit, or multisynaptic circuit**.
- For lesions, state the relationship to relevant **decussations** and nearby fiber systems.
- For cortical pathways, give laminar origin/termination only where supported; do not force every pathway into a layer-II/III or layer-IV template.
- For physiology, distinguish **direct pathway-specific evidence** from network-level EEG/MEG/iEEG correlations.
- For basal-ganglia loops, do not omit intervening **GPe, GPi, STN, SNr/SNc, ventral pallidum, or thalamic** stages when mechanistically relevant.
- For hippocampal circuitry, distinguish **large white-matter output tracts** (e.g., fornix) from **intrinsic fibers** (perforant path, mossy fibers, Schaffer collaterals, temporoammonic pathway).
- For cerebellar circuitry, distinguish **afferent peduncles/pathways, deep-nuclear efferents, and closed cerebro-cerebellar loops**.
- When evidence derives mainly from nonhuman tracing or physiology, label that translation explicitly.

## Cerebral projection, commissural, association, and local cortical fibers

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Corticospinal tract (CST) | Descending projection tract | Primary motor cortex with contributions from premotor/SMA and somatosensory cortex | Corona radiata → posterior limb internal capsule → cerebral peduncle → ventral pons → medullary pyramids; most fibers cross at pyramidal decussation | Spinal interneurons and motor-neuron pools, strongest direct corticomotoneuronal access for distal dexterous muscles | Predominantly crossed; smaller anterior/uncrossed component | Mainly layer V corticofugal pyramidal neurons, including Betz cells in M1 | Voluntary skilled movement; movement-related beta desynchronization/rebound is a corticospinal-network correlate, not an intrinsic tract rhythm | Established: human anatomy, lesions, TMS/MEP, diffusion MRI |
| Corticobulbar / corticonuclear tract | Descending projection system | Face/tongue/laryngeal motor cortex and adjacent premotor cortex | Corona radiata → genu/internal capsule → cerebral peduncle → brainstem motor networks | Especially trigeminal motor nucleus, facial nucleus, nucleus ambiguus, hypoglossal nucleus; ocular-motor control uses partly separate corticobrainstem gaze networks | Bilateral for many targets; clinically important asymmetric contralateral dominance for lower facial and genioglossal systems | Predominantly layer V pyramidal neurons | Speech, swallowing, facial and orolingual voluntary control; beta desynchronization accompanies movement | Established; target pattern is more selective than a blanket 'III–XII' description |
| Thalamocortical radiations | Projection system | Specific and association thalamic nuclei | Internal capsule/corona radiata; topography depends on anterior, superior, posterior and inferior thalamic peduncles | Primary sensory, motor and association cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral | Thalamic relay neurons; strong sensory terminations in layer IV, with substantial layer I/III/V/VI projections depending nucleus and cortical area | Feedforward and recurrent thalamocortical signaling; alpha, spindles, delta and gamma coupling depend on nucleus/state | Established; laminar pattern is nucleus- and cortex-specific |
| Corticothalamic projections | Projection system | Neocortex | Descending through corona radiata/internal capsule to thalamus | Thalamic relay, association and reticular nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral | Layer VI corticothalamic pyramidal neurons dominate; layer V 'driver' projections to higher-order thalamus also occur | Feedback/gain control, predictive and state-dependent thalamocortical regulation; alpha/delta/spindle interactions | Established |
| Corpus callosum | Commissural fasciculus | Widespread neocortex | Rostrum/genu/body/isthmus/splenium according to topography | Contralateral homotopic and heterotopic cortex | Commissural | Callosal pyramidal neurons are common in layers II/III with contributions from deeper layers depending area | Interhemispheric sensory, motor and cognitive integration; alpha/gamma/beta coherence varies by task | Established |
| Anterior commissure | Commissural fasciculus | Temporal, olfactory and amygdalar/limbic territories | Crosses in anterior wall region near lamina terminalis | Contralateral temporal/olfactory/amygdalar regions | Commissural | Long-range telencephalic projection axons; cortical laminar origin varies | Interhemispheric temporal and olfactory/limbic integration | Established |
| Posterior commissure | Brainstem commissure | Pretectal and rostral midbrain/interstitial nuclei | Dorsal rostral midbrain above the cerebral aqueduct | Contralateral pretectal/midbrain targets | Commissural | Mixed commissural projection neurons | Pupillary light-reflex and vertical-gaze related interconnections | Established: human lesion anatomy and classical neuroanatomy |
| Superior longitudinal fasciculus (SLF I–III) | Association fasciculus family | Frontal and parietal association cortex | Longitudinal dorsal hemispheric white matter; subdivisions differ in dorsal/ventral position and cortical endpoints | Reciprocal frontal–parietal territories | Ipsilateral; bilateral homologues | Corticocortical pyramidal projection neurons; not restricted to a single cortical layer | Attention, visuospatial processing, working memory, motor planning and language-related functions by subdivision; beta/gamma/theta-gamma network coupling | Established; exact subdivision nomenclature/endpoints vary across methods |
| Arcuate fasciculus | Association fasciculus / dorsal language-network component | Posterior temporal/inferior parietal and frontal language-related territories | Curves around posterior Sylvian region as part of the dorsal perisylvian association system | Reciprocal temporo-frontal/frontoparietal targets | Ipsilateral; typically left-lateralized for many language functions | Long-range corticocortical pyramidal axons; not a one-way Wernicke→Broca wire | Phonological processing, speech repetition, sensorimotor language integration; task-related beta/gamma/theta effects | Established; human dissection, tractography, stimulation; segmentation varies |
| Inferior fronto-occipital fasciculus (IFOF) | Association fasciculus | Occipital/posterior temporal-parietal association territories | Deep ventral pathway through temporal stem/external-extreme capsular region | Ventrolateral/orbitofrontal and other frontal association cortex | Ipsilateral | Long-range corticocortical pyramidal axons; tract-specific laminar composition not uniquely established | Semantic, visual-associative and language-network integration; oscillatory effects are network-level | Established: human dissection, diffusion MRI, intraoperative stimulation |
| Inferior longitudinal fasciculus (ILF) | Association fasciculus | Occipital visual association cortex | Long ventral occipitotemporal white matter | Posterior/anterior temporal and ventral temporal cortex | Ipsilateral | Corticocortical projection axons | Visual object/face processing and ventral visual-stream integration; alpha/gamma interactions at network level | Established |
| Middle longitudinal fasciculus (MdLF) | Association fasciculus | Superior temporal and temporal association cortex | Longitudinal temporoparietal white matter, superior to ILF | Inferior parietal and posterior association territories; proposed occipital extensions vary | Ipsilateral | Long-range corticocortical projection axons | Auditory-language, semantic and higher-order association functions; no single validated tract-specific rhythm | Established with unresolved subdivisions/endpoints |
| Uncinate fasciculus | Association fasciculus | Anterior temporal cortex/amygdalar-temporal pole region | Hooks through temporal stem around Sylvian stem | Orbitofrontal/ventral prefrontal cortex | Ipsilateral | Long-range corticocortical/limbic projection axons | Emotion-memory, valuation and semantic/person-knowledge integration; theta-gamma effects are network-level | Established |
| Cingulum bundle | Association/limbic fasciculus | Cingulate, medial frontal, parietal and parahippocampal territories | Runs within cingulate gyrus around corpus callosum into parahippocampal white matter | Reciprocal medial cortical and medial temporal/limbic targets | Ipsilateral; bilateral homologues | Mixed long- and short-range corticocortical fibers from superficial and deep pyramidal populations | Memory, attention, cognitive-emotional integration; theta/alpha/beta coupling in distributed limbic networks | Established; heterogeneous fiber composition |
| Frontal aslant tract (FAT) | Association fasciculus | Inferior frontal/ventrolateral frontal cortex | Oblique frontal white matter | pre-SMA/SMA and adjacent superior frontal cortex | Ipsilateral; bilateral homologues | Corticocortical projection neurons; layer specificity not uniquely established | Speech initiation/fluency, motor initiation and inhibitory control; beta/theta-gamma effects are network-level | Established: human dissection, tractography, intraoperative stimulation |
| Vertical occipital fasciculus (VOF) | Association fasciculus | Dorsal occipital/parietal visual territories | Vertical posterior occipital white matter lateral to major sagittal systems | Ventral occipitotemporal territories | Ipsilateral | Corticocortical projection axons | Dorsal–ventral visual-stream integration, reading and visual recognition | Established |
| Short U-fibers | Local association fibers | Adjacent cortical gyri | Subcortical white matter immediately beneath sulci | Neighboring gyri | Ipsilateral | Corticocortical pyramidal axons from multiple layers, especially superficial layers | Local cortical communication and high-frequency coordination; high-gamma associations are network-level | Established; dense and individually variable |
| Interlaminar intracortical fibers | Local cortical microcircuit fibers | Superficial and deep cortical neurons within/among columns | Horizontal and vertical intracortical trajectories | Nearby and sometimes more distant cortical columns/layers | Local | Pyramidal axons plus diverse interneuron axons (including Martinotti, basket, double-bouquet and other classes depending layer/area) | Columnar integration; beta/gamma propagation emerges from recurrent excitation/inhibition rather than a unitary tract | Established at microcircuit level; human cellular detail supplemented by comparative physiology |

## Ascending sensory, sensory-relay, and pain-control pathways

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Dorsal column–medial lemniscus (DCML) | Ascending sensory pathway | Primary afferents from dorsal-root ganglia | Fasciculi gracilis/cuneatus → gracile/cuneate nuclei → internal arcuate fibers decussate → medial lemniscus → VPL thalamus | Primary somatosensory cortex and related parietal targets via thalamocortical projections | Body afferents ascend ipsilaterally in cord, then cross in caudal medulla | Large myelinated primary afferents; glutamatergic brainstem/thalamic relays; cortical input strongly engages layer IV | Fine touch, vibration, conscious proprioception; somatosensory alpha/beta modulation | Established |
| Anterolateral / spinothalamic system | Ascending sensory pathway family | Spinal dorsal-horn projection neurons | Crosses segmentally in anterior white commissure; ascends anterolaterally with thalamic and brainstem branches | VPL/VPI, posterior/intralaminar thalamic nuclei and cortical pain/somatosensory networks via relays | Predominantly contralateral with bilateral components | Glutamatergic spinal projection neurons; multiple relay cell types | Pain, temperature, crude touch and salience; pain-related theta/gamma/alpha changes are distributed-network phenomena | Established; broader than a simple dorsal horn→VPL→S1 chain |
| Spinoreticular pathway | Ascending nociceptive pathway | Spinal dorsal horn / anterolateral-system neurons | Anterolateral cord → medullary/pontine reticular formation → intralaminar thalamic/forebrain routes | Diffuse thalamic and forebrain targets | Crossed and bilateral | Glutamatergic spinal projection neurons with reticular relays | Arousal/affective dimension of pain; slow-wave/theta/gamma changes are state dependent | Established |
| Spinomesencephalic pathway | Ascending nociceptive pathway | Spinal dorsal horn / anterolateral-system neurons | Anterolateral cord → PAG, superior colliculus and other midbrain targets | Midbrain pain-control/orienting networks | Predominantly crossed with bilateral components | Glutamatergic spinal projection neurons | Pain modulation, orienting and defensive responses | Established |
| Optic nerve–chiasm–tract system | Sensory projection system | Retinal ganglion cells | Optic nerve → optic chiasm → optic tract; collateral branches to pretectum, superior colliculus and hypothalamus | LGN plus non-geniculate visual/reflex/circadian targets | Nasal retinal fibers cross; temporal retinal fibers remain ipsilateral | Retinal ganglion-cell axons; predominantly glutamatergic | Image-forming vision, orienting, pupillary and circadian input; visual evoked potentials assess system-level conduction | Established |
| Optic radiations | Thalamocortical sensory projection | LGN | Retrolenticular internal capsule; temporal Meyer loop and parietal bundles | Primary visual cortex (V1) | Ipsilateral representation of contralateral visual hemifield | Thalamic relay axons terminate strongly in V1 layer IVc with additional laminar targets | Visual cortical activation, alpha desynchronization and gamma synchronization | Established |
| Brainstem auditory pathway / lateral lemniscus system | Multisynaptic sensory pathway | Cochlear nuclei | Acoustic striae/trapezoid-body pathways → superior olivary complex and/or lateral-lemniscus nuclei → inferior colliculus → brachium of IC → MGN | Auditory thalamus and cortex via auditory radiations | Strong bilateral organization with early crossings; not every relay is obligatory for every fiber | Glutamatergic and inhibitory relay neurons specialized for temporal/binaural coding | Sound timing/intensity, binaural localization and auditory perception; auditory brainstem responses directly assay early pathway timing | Established |
| Auditory radiations | Thalamocortical sensory projection | Medial geniculate nucleus | Sublenticular internal capsule | Primary auditory cortex on Heschl gyri and adjacent auditory cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral | Thalamic relay axons; strong middle-layer terminations | Auditory perception; gamma/theta tracking of acoustic and speech structure | Established |
| Olfactory tract and primary olfactory projections | Sensory projection system | Mitral/tufted cells of olfactory bulb | Lateral olfactory tract and associated olfactory pathways | Piriform cortex, amygdalar/entorhinal and related olfactory territories | Predominantly ipsilateral with commissural integration downstream | Mitral/tufted glutamatergic axons to allocortical/paleocortical targets | Odor identity, valence and memory; respiration-coupled theta/low-frequency oscillations | Established; primary olfactory cortex is reached without an obligatory first thalamic relay |
| Trigeminal sensory systems | Multisynaptic cranial sensory system | Trigeminal ganglion and mesencephalic trigeminal neurons | Principal sensory nucleus, spinal trigeminal nucleus and mesencephalic nucleus → trigeminothalamic pathways | VPM thalamus → face somatosensory cortex; brainstem reflex networks | Predominantly crossed trigeminothalamic output with important ipsilateral components | Primary sensory neurons plus glutamatergic brainstem relay neurons | Facial touch, pain, temperature and proprioception; somatosensory evoked potentials and distributed pain rhythms | Established |
| Solitary tract / viscerosensory pathway | Cranial visceral sensory system | Visceral afferents traveling mainly in VII, IX and X | Solitary tract → nucleus tractus solitarius → parabrachial/autonomic/hypothalamic/thalamic relays | Hypothalamus, amygdala, insular/cingulate cortex and autonomic brainstem targets | Initially mainly ipsilateral; higher-order representation is bilateral | Glutamatergic primary/relay neurons with peptide-rich autonomic circuits | Homeostasis, baroreception and interoception; cortical signals couple to cardiac/respiratory rhythms | Established |
| Gustatory pathway | Multisynaptic sensory pathway | Taste afferents in VII, IX and X | Rostral NTS and species-dependent brainstem organization → thalamic relay | VPMpc → insular/frontal opercular gustatory cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral at higher levels | Primary gustatory afferents and glutamatergic relay neurons | Taste quality and valuation; cortical theta/gamma responses | Established; exact intermediate relay organization differs across species |
| Vestibulocortical pathways | Ascending sensory projection system | Vestibular nerve and vestibular nuclei | Brainstem → thalamic relays | Parieto-insular vestibular cortex (PIVC), parietal operculum and distributed multisensory cortex | Bilateral, with pathway-specific asymmetries | Vestibular relay neurons → thalamocortical projections | Self-motion, spatial orientation and balance perception; theta/alpha network modulation | Established; distributed rather than a single unitary tract |
| Descending pain modulatory system | Multisynaptic modulatory circuit | PAG plus forebrain pain-control inputs | PAG → rostral ventromedial medulla/related pontomedullary nuclei → descending spinal/trigeminal routes | Spinal and trigeminal dorsal horn | Bilateral | Opioidergic, serotonergic, glutamatergic and GABAergic components; ON/OFF-cell physiology in RVM | Endogenous analgesia and facilitation of pain; state-dependent autonomic/theta/gamma coupling | Established: human stimulation/imaging/pharmacology plus extensive comparative physiology |

## Descending motor, brainstem, vestibular, and orienting systems

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Vestibulospinal system | Descending brainstem tract family | Vestibular nuclei | Lateral and medial vestibulospinal components descend through ventromedial spinal pathways | Axial/proximal spinal interneurons and motor-neuron pools; cervical targets prominent for medial component | Mostly ipsilateral for lateral component; bilateral for medial component | Excitatory and inhibitory vestibular projection neurons depending component | Posture, extensor tone, head/neck stabilization and balance | Established |
| Reticulospinal system | Descending brainstem tract family | Pontine and medullary reticular formation | Ventromedial and lateral brainstem/spinal trajectories | Spinal interneurons and motor-neuron networks | Bilateral with ipsilateral biases depending component | Mixed glutamatergic/GABAergic/glycinergic brainstem projections | Posture, locomotion, anticipatory adjustments and recovery of movement after corticospinal injury; alpha/beta coherence may reflect distributed motor networks | Established |
| Corticoreticular projection | Descending corticobrainstem projection | Frontal motor/premotor and parietal cortex | Corona radiata/internal capsule → pontomedullary reticular formation | Reticular formation, feeding reticulospinal and autonomic networks | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral cortical control | Mainly deep-layer corticofugal pyramidal neurons | Cortical control of posture, locomotion, muscle tone and anticipatory adjustments | Established; human tractography/lesion evidence with comparative tracing |
| Rubrospinal tract | Descending tract | Red nucleus, especially magnocellular region | Crosses in ventral tegmental decussation → lateral brainstem/spinal cord | Predominantly cervical spinal interneuronal circuits | Contralateral after decussation | Red-nucleus projection neurons | Upper-limb motor control; much less dominant in adult humans than in many nonhuman mammals | Established but relatively small in humans |
| Tectospinal tract | Descending tract | Superior colliculus | Crosses in dorsal tegmental decussation → medial brainstem → cervical spinal cord | Cervical interneurons controlling head/neck orientation | Contralateral after crossing | Collicular projection neurons | Reflexive orienting of head/neck toward salient stimuli | Established |
| Medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF) | Brainstem longitudinal fasciculus | Vestibular nuclei, abducens internuclear neurons and rostral interstitial ocular-motor nuclei | Paramedian dorsal brainstem near fourth ventricle/cerebral aqueduct | III, IV and VI nuclei; cervical spinal/vestibular-related targets | Bilateral with crossed and uncrossed components | Mixed excitatory/inhibitory internuclear projection neurons | Conjugate gaze, vestibulo-ocular reflex and head-eye coordination; lesions cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia | Established |
| Central tegmental tract | Brainstem tract | Red nucleus and other brainstem sources | Central tegmentum through midbrain/pons; includes an important rubro-olivary component | Inferior olive and other tegmental targets | Mostly ipsilateral for rubro-olivary component | Mixed projection systems | Motor-learning/olivocerebellar modulation and brainstem integration | Established; heterogeneous tract composition |
| Corticovestibular projections | Descending corticobrainstem projection | PIVC/posterior insula and related parietal vestibular cortex | Descending through deep hemispheric/brainstem pathways | Vestibular nuclei | Bilateral/ipsilateral components | Deep-layer corticofugal pyramidal neurons | Top-down modulation of balance, self-motion and spatial orientation | Established at system level; exact human tract segmentation is less standardized |
| Visual corticocollicular projection | Descending corticofugal sensory projection | Visual cortex V1–V3 and higher visual areas | Deep white matter/internal-capsular/brainstem routes | Superior colliculus | Predominantly ipsilateral with retinotopic organization | Mainly deep-layer corticofugal pyramidal neurons | Visual orienting, attention and modulation of collicular sensorimotor maps | Established |
| Auditory corticocollicular projection | Descending corticofugal sensory projection | Auditory cortex | Descending auditory pathways through subcortical white matter/brainstem | Inferior colliculus | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral network effects | Deep-layer corticofugal pyramidal neurons | Top-down auditory gain, localization and predictive modulation | Established |
| Corticorubral projection / corticospinal-collateral component | Descending corticofugal projection | Motor and premotor cortex | Corticofugal fibers branch toward midbrain red nucleus | Red nucleus | Predominantly ipsilateral cortical projection before rubrospinal crossing | Layer V corticofugal pyramidal neurons; some axons are collateralized with other descending projections | Motor timing, coordination and cortical influence on rubral systems | Established; 'corticospinal collateral' is not the only anatomical source |

## Cerebellar afferent, efferent, and cerebro-cerebellar loop systems

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Corticopontocerebellar system | Multisynaptic corticocerebellar pathway | Motor, premotor, parietal and frontal cortex | Corticopontine fibers → ipsilateral pontine nuclei → crossed pontocerebellar fibers through middle cerebellar peduncle | Contralateral cerebellar cortex | Cortical leg ipsilateral to pons; pontocerebellar leg crosses | Layer V corticopontine pyramidal neurons → glutamatergic pontine mossy-fiber system | Motor coordination, prediction and cognitive cerebellar communication; beta/low-gamma effects are network-level | Established |
| Frontopontine tract | Descending corticopontine projection | Prefrontal/frontal cortex | Anterior limb/internal capsule and medial cerebral peduncle → pontine nuclei | Pontine nuclei, then cerebellum through crossed pontocerebellar fibers | Ipsilateral cortex→pons; crossed pons→cerebellum | Layer V corticofugal pyramidal neurons | Executive/cognitive input to cerebellar loops | Established |
| Temporopontine tract | Descending corticopontine projection | Temporal cortex | Sublenticular/retrolenticular and lateral cerebral-peduncular routes → pontine nuclei | Pontine nuclei → contralateral cerebellum | Ipsilateral cortex→pons; crossed pons→cerebellum | Layer V corticofugal pyramidal neurons | Auditory/language/temporal association input to cerebellar processing | Established at tract-family level; precise topography varies |
| Dorsal spinocerebellar tract | Ascending cerebellar tract | Clarke nucleus / spinal proprioceptive circuitry | Lateral funiculus → inferior cerebellar peduncle | Ipsilateral cerebellum | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic spinal projection neurons | Unconscious proprioception and spinal-state information | Established |
| Ventral spinocerebellar tract | Ascending cerebellar tract | Spinal border cells/interneuronal networks | Crosses in spinal cord → ascends anterolaterally → superior cerebellar peduncle; many fibers recross/terminate functionally ipsilateral | Cerebellum | Double-crossing tendency | Spinal projection neurons | Internal copy of spinal locomotor/interneuronal state | Established |
| Cuneocerebellar tract | Ascending cerebellar tract | Accessory cuneate nucleus | Inferior cerebellar peduncle | Ipsilateral cerebellum | Ipsilateral | Glutamatergic relay neurons | Upper-limb/neck proprioceptive information | Established |
| Rostral spinocerebellar pathway | Ascending cerebellar system | Cervical spinal interneuronal/projection populations | Superior and inferior cerebellar-peduncular components | Cerebellum | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral components | Spinal projection neurons | Upper-limb spinal-network state and proprioceptive integration | Established; detailed human topography less complete than in experimental animals |
| Olivocerebellar pathway | Projection system | Inferior olivary nucleus | Crosses and enters via inferior cerebellar peduncle | Contralateral cerebellar cortex as climbing fibers | Strongly crossed | Glutamatergic climbing fibers → Purkinje cells | Complex spikes, timing/error signals and motor learning; olivary synchrony shapes cerebellar timing | Established |
| Vestibulocerebellar pathways | Projection system | Vestibular nerve and vestibular nuclei | Inferior cerebellar peduncle / juxtarestiform body | Flocculonodular lobe, vermis and related cerebellar territories | Mostly ipsilateral with bilateral organization | Primary vestibular afferents and vestibular projection neurons | Balance, gaze stabilization and spatial orientation | Established |
| Cerebellovestibular pathways | Projection system | Purkinje cells and fastigial/deep cerebellar outputs | Direct Purkinje projections and inferior-peduncular/juxtarestiform routes | Vestibular nuclei | Bilateral/ipsilateral depending component | GABAergic Purkinje output plus glutamatergic deep-nuclear projections | Posture, equilibrium and vestibulo-ocular calibration | Established |
| Cerebellothalamic / dentatothalamic system | Ascending cerebellar output | Dentate and other deep cerebellar nuclei | Superior cerebellar peduncle → major decussation in caudal midbrain → ventral/association thalamus | VA/VL and other thalamic nuclei → motor/premotor/prefrontal cortex | Predominantly crossed cerebellar output | Glutamatergic deep-nuclear projection neurons → thalamic relay neurons | Motor adaptation, timing and cerebellar cognitive output; theta/beta effects reflect loop activity | Established; cortical termination cannot be reduced to a universal layer-IV rule |
| Frontocerebellar cognitive loop | Multisynaptic closed loop | DLPFC | DLPFC → pontine nuclei → cerebellum (including dentate) → thalamus → DLPFC | DLPFC | Crossed corticopontocerebellar and cerebellothalamic legs yield looped hemispheric relationships | Cortical pyramidal → pontine mossy fibers → Purkinje/deep nuclei → thalamic relay | Working memory and executive control; theta/beta coupling reported at network level | Established network architecture; exact closed-loop segregation is not absolute |
| Cingulo-cerebellar loop | Multisynaptic closed loop | Dorsal ACC/cingulate motor-affective regions | Corticopontine → cerebellar → thalamic return | ACC/cingulate cortex | Crossed subcortical legs within a bilateral network | Cortical pyramidal, pontine, cerebellar and thalamic relays | Error monitoring, affective and autonomic regulation; theta/beta network effects | Probable/established at network level; precise tract segmentation less standardized |

## Basal-ganglia output tracts and corticostriatal loop architecture

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Nigrostriatal pathway | Neuromodulatory projection | Substantia nigra pars compacta | Ascends through medial forebrain/substantia nigra–striatal projection routes | Dorsal striatum (caudate and putamen) | Predominantly ipsilateral | Dopaminergic axons modulating D1/D2-family receptor-expressing striatal neurons | Movement, reinforcement learning and action vigor; excessive beta synchrony is characteristic of parkinsonian basal-ganglia networks | Established |
| Direct basal-ganglia pathway | Multisynaptic circuit | Cortex → striatum | D1-enriched striatal MSNs → GPi/SNr → thalamus | Thalamocortical return to frontal/motor/association cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral loop architecture | Glutamatergic cortex; GABAergic striatal MSNs; GABAergic GPi/SNr | Action selection/gating; frequency dynamics are task- and disease-state dependent | Established canonical abstraction; real circuitry includes collateral/recurrent pathways |
| Indirect basal-ganglia pathway | Multisynaptic circuit | Cortex → striatum | D2-enriched MSNs → GPe → STN → GPi/SNr → thalamus | Thalamocortical return | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic cortex/STN; GABAergic striatum/GPe/GPi/SNr | Action suppression/selection; pathological beta synchrony prominent in Parkinson disease | Established canonical abstraction |
| Hyperdirect corticosubthalamic pathway | Projection/circuit component | Motor, premotor and prefrontal cortex | Corticofugal fibers → STN → GPi/SNr | Basal-ganglia output and downstream thalamocortical loops | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic cortical projection neurons → glutamatergic STN neurons | Rapid stopping/conflict control; fast cortical-STN interactions often involve beta-range activity | Established: human DBS evoked potentials/electrophysiology, imaging, NHP tracing |
| Ansa lenticularis | Pallidothalamic tract | GPi | Ventral sweep around internal capsule toward fields of Forel | Thalamus via fasciculus thalamicus | Ipsilateral | GABAergic pallidal projection axons | Major basal-ganglia output route | Established; surgical/DBS landmark |
| Fasciculus lenticularis | Pallidothalamic tract | GPi | Traverses/penetrates subthalamic region dorsal to STN → fields of Forel | Thalamus via fasciculus thalamicus | Ipsilateral | GABAergic pallidal projection axons | Major basal-ganglia output route | Established |
| Fasciculus thalamicus (H1 field of Forel) | Convergent thalamic approach tract | Pallidal and cerebellar output fibers after convergence in subthalamic region | Fields of Forel | VA/VL and related thalamic nuclei | Ipsilateral for pallidal component; crossed origin for many cerebellar fibers before convergence | Mixed GABAergic pallidal and glutamatergic cerebellar fibers by component | Final common subthalamic approach of major motor outputs to thalamus | Established; clinically important DBS/lesion target region |
| Nigrothalamic projection | Basal-ganglia output projection | Substantia nigra pars reticulata | Ascending ventral diencephalic routes | Motor/association thalamic nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral | GABAergic SNr projection neurons | Basal-ganglia output to thalamus and action/oculomotor gating | Established |
| Thalamostriatal projections | Projection system | Intralaminar thalamus, especially centromedian/parafascicular complex; additional thalamic nuclei contribute | Deep hemispheric white matter | Striatum | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic thalamic neurons → MSNs and striatal interneurons | Salience, action switching, attention and learning | Established; detailed synaptic topography strongly informed by NHP tracing |
| Primary motor corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | M1 (area 4) | Posterolateral putamen → pallidal/nigral output → VA/VL thalamus | Motor cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral loop | Deep-layer cortical glutamatergic projections → striatal MSNs | Voluntary movement; beta/gamma dynamics | Established topographic loop |
| Premotor corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | Premotor cortex (area 6) | Dorsolateral putamen → basal-ganglia output → VA/VL | Premotor cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer cortical projections → striatum | Motor sequencing and selection; beta/gamma network dynamics | Established |
| SMA / pre-SMA corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | SMA and pre-SMA | Dorsomedial putamen/caudate border territories → basal-ganglia output → VA/VL/MD | SMA/pre-SMA | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer cortical projections → striatum | Internally generated action, sequencing and switching; theta/beta interactions | Established |
| Oculomotor corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | FEF/SEF | Caudate → SNr/GPi → MD/VA and superior-collicular-related output networks | Frontal eye fields / supplementary eye fields | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer cortical projections; GABAergic striatal output | Saccade selection and suppression; beta/theta dynamics | Established |
| DLPFC corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | DLPFC (BA9/46) | Dorsolateral caudate → pallidal/nigral output → MD thalamus | DLPFC | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer cortical projections → striatum | Working memory, planning and cognitive control; frontal theta/beta | Established |
| Orbitofrontal corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | OFC (BA11/12/13) | Ventromedial caudate/ventral striatum → basal-ganglia output → MD/VA | OFC | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer cortical projections → striatum | Reward valuation, reversal learning and outcome updating; theta/alpha changes | Established |
| ACC corticostriatal loop | Topographic frontostriatal loop | Dorsal ACC (BA24/32) | Ventral/dorsomedial striatal territories → basal-ganglia output → MD | ACC | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer cortical projections → striatum | Conflict monitoring, effort and motivation; midfrontal theta | Established |
| Limbic / vmPFC corticostriatal loop | Topographic limbic basal-ganglia loop | vmPFC and subgenual ACC | Nucleus accumbens/ventral striatum → ventral pallidal/midbrain outputs → MD/VA | vmPFC/limbic frontal cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral limbic integration | Glutamatergic cortical projections → ventral-striatal MSNs | Mood, reward and affective regulation; delta/theta network dynamics | Established |
| Inferior frontal–striatal language/articulatory loop | Topographic corticostriatal loop | IFG BA44/45 and adjacent speech motor cortex | Anterior/ventrolateral putamen → basal-ganglia output → VA/related thalamus | Inferior frontal/speech motor cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral; function often left-dominant | Corticostriatal glutamatergic projections | Articulation and speech motor control; beta/gamma dynamics | Established at network/topographic level |
| Ventrolateral prefrontal corticostriatal loop | Topographic corticostriatal loop | VLPFC/IFG BA47/12 | Ventral putamen/caudate → basal-ganglia output → VA/MD | VLPFC | Predominantly ipsilateral | Corticostriatal glutamatergic projections | Response inhibition and rule selection; theta/beta suppression | Established |
| Parietal–striatal loop | Corticostriatal association loop | Posterior parietal cortex (BA5/7 and related areas) | Dorsal striatal territories → basal-ganglia output → VA/VL/association thalamus | Parietal/frontal sensorimotor networks | Predominantly ipsilateral | Corticostriatal glutamatergic projections | Sensorimotor integration and action selection; beta/gamma dynamics | Established at system level |
| Temporal–striatal loop | Corticostriatal association loop | Temporal pole and temporal association cortex | Ventral/anterior caudate/striatum → basal-ganglia output → MD | Temporal/prefrontal association networks | Predominantly ipsilateral | Corticostriatal glutamatergic projections | Semantic and social-cognitive integration; theta/alpha network dynamics | Established at system level; precise human topography variable |
| Insular–striatal loop | Corticostriatal association loop | Anterior/mid insula | Ventral striatum/putamen → basal-ganglia output → MD/VA | Insular/prefrontal networks | Predominantly ipsilateral | Corticostriatal glutamatergic projections | Interoception, salience and motivated action; theta/alpha dynamics | Established at system level |
| Subgenual ACC–VTA–accumbens circuit | Mesocorticolimbic circuit | Subgenual ACC/vmPFC | VTA ↔ nucleus accumbens shell and ventral pallidal/thalamic return pathways | vmPFC/subgenual and broader limbic networks | Bilateral/ipsilateral components | Glutamatergic cortical, dopaminergic VTA and GABAergic striatal/pallidal components | Mood, motivation and reward regulation; delta/theta interactions | Established network; not a single monosynaptic closed tract |
| Amygdalo–striatal pathway | Limbic projection | Basolateral amygdala | Direct projections to ventral striatum/nucleus accumbens with downstream pallidal/thalamic loops | Ventral striatum/nucleus accumbens | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral limbic network | Predominantly glutamatergic amygdalar projection neurons onto striatal cells | Emotional salience, reinforcement and motivational gating; theta/gamma coupling | Established |
| Motor corticostriatal projection / corticofugal collateral component | Corticostriatal projection | Motor cortex | Deep hemispheric white matter to putamen; some projection neurons may collateralize with descending motor systems | Putamen | Predominantly ipsilateral | Deep-layer glutamatergic corticofugal/corticostriatal neurons | Motor efference-copy/action-selection signaling; beta/gamma interactions | Established; avoid assuming all such fibers are literal corticospinal collaterals |

## Hippocampal–diencephalic, amygdalar, and habenular systems

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Fornix (alveus–fimbria–crus–body–columns) | Limbic fasciculus / projection system | Hippocampal formation, especially subiculum and hippocampal fields | Alveus → fimbria → crura → body → columns; divides into precommissural and postcommissural components | Septal/basal-forebrain regions, mammillary bodies, hypothalamic and related limbic targets | Predominantly ipsilateral with commissural components | Glutamatergic hippocampal projection neurons; downstream targets heterogeneous | Episodic memory and hippocampal–diencephalic communication; hippocampal theta couples across this network | Established: human dissection/histology, diffusion MRI, lesion/DBS observations |
| Mammillothalamic tract (MTT) | Limbic tract | Mammillary bodies | Ascends beside third ventricle through diencephalon | Anterior thalamic nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral | Projection neurons from mammillary nuclei | Papez-circuit memory transmission; theta-network interactions | Established |
| Mammillotegmental tract | Limbic/brainstem tract | Mammillary bodies | Descends into midbrain/brainstem tegmentum | Tegmental nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral | Projection-neuron composition incompletely characterized in humans | Limbic–brainstem/autonomic coupling | Established; less completely mapped in humans |
| Dorsal hippocampal commissure / commissure of fornix | Commissural limbic system | Medial temporal/hippocampal-related territories | Fibers beneath splenial/forniceal region | Contralateral medial temporal structures | Commissural | Long-range medial temporal projection axons | Interhemispheric medial temporal integration | Established but relatively small/complex in humans; differs from prominent rodent hippocampal commissure |
| Perforant path | Entorhinal–hippocampal projection | Entorhinal cortex | Perforates subicular region to hippocampal formation | Dentate gyrus and hippocampal fields | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic entorhinal projection neurons | Major cortical input to hippocampus; theta-gamma phase organization supports episodic encoding | Established |
| Dentate mossy fibers | Intrinsic hippocampal projection | Dentate granule cells | Hilus → CA3 stratum lucidum | CA3 pyramidal cells and interneurons | Ipsilateral | Glutamatergic granule-cell axons with giant mossy terminals | Pattern separation and sparse encoding; participates in theta/gamma and sharp-wave-ripple circuitry | Established |
| Schaffer collaterals | Intrinsic hippocampal projection | CA3 pyramidal neurons | CA3 → CA1 through stratum radiatum/oriens | CA1 pyramidal neurons and interneurons | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic pyramidal-cell axons | Associative memory and CA3→CA1 transfer; theta/gamma and sharp-wave-ripple propagation | Established |
| Temporoammonic pathway | Entorhinal–hippocampal projection | Entorhinal cortex | Direct input through hippocampal molecular-layer routes | Distal CA1 dendrites and subiculum | Predominantly ipsilateral | Glutamatergic entorhinal projection neurons | Direct cortical input to CA1/subiculum; context and memory integration with theta-phase dependence | Established |
| Direct hippocampo–prefrontal pathway | Limbic corticocortical projection | Ventral/anterior hippocampus and subiculum | Long-range limbic fibers through medial forebrain/forniceal-cingulate-related routes depending component | Medial prefrontal cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral network effects | Glutamatergic hippocampal pyramidal projection neurons | Memory–executive coupling; hippocampal–prefrontal theta synchrony | Established at system level; human tract course less sharply delimited than in rodents |
| Stria terminalis | Limbic tract | Central/medial amygdalar complex | C-shaped course along caudate/ventricular margin toward septal/hypothalamic region | Bed nucleus of stria terminalis, septal and hypothalamic targets | Predominantly ipsilateral | Predominantly GABAergic amygdalar output with peptide components | Sustained threat, stress and autonomic/endocrine regulation; slow/theta network effects | Established |
| Ventral amygdalofugal pathway | Limbic projection system | Basolateral/central amygdalar regions | Diffuse ventral basal-forebrain/substantia-innominata route | Hypothalamus, septal/basal forebrain, thalamus and brainstem targets | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral network effects | Mixed glutamatergic and GABAergic components depending amygdalar nucleus/target | Rapid emotional-autonomic and motivational output | Established |
| Amygdalo–hypothalamic projections | Limbic-autonomic projection system | Central and basolateral amygdalar nuclei | Via stria terminalis and ventral amygdalofugal routes | Hypothalamic nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral | Mixed GABAergic/glutamatergic/peptidergic pathways by nucleus | Emotional-autonomic/endocrine regulation; theta-linked limbic activity | Established |
| Amygdalo–prefrontal projections | Limbic corticocortical projection | Basolateral amygdala | Ventral/anterior temporal–frontal pathways including uncinate-related white matter | OFC, vmPFC and medial frontal cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral network | Predominantly glutamatergic projection neurons; cortical targets engage pyramidal/interneuron circuits | Emotional salience, valuation and regulation; amygdala–PFC theta synchrony | Established |
| Corticohabenular projections | Limbic/diencephalic projection | ACC, mPFC and related forebrain regions | Often reach habenula through stria medullaris and related pathways | Lateral and/or medial habenular territories depending source | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral components | Glutamatergic forebrain projection neurons | Reward prediction, aversion and behavioral control; low-frequency/theta activity | Established at system level; exact human topography less resolved than animal tracing |
| Stria medullaris thalami | Limbic/habenular tract | Septal/basal forebrain, hypothalamic and other forebrain inputs | Runs along dorsomedial thalamus toward epithalamus | Habenular complex | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral components | Mixed forebrain projection neurons | Conveys limbic/reward/aversive information to habenula | Established |
| Medial habenula → interpeduncular pathway (fasciculus retroflexus) | Epithalamic tract | Medial habenula | Fasciculus retroflexus descends through midbrain | Interpeduncular nucleus | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral organization | Mainly glutamatergic/cholinergic medial-habenular projection populations depending subnucleus | Aversion, nicotine-related signaling, mood and state regulation | Established; this corrects the legacy misassignment of the canonical IPN projection to lateral habenula |
| Lateral habenular efferents | Epithalamic projection system | Lateral habenula | Fasciculus retroflexus and adjacent midbrain routes | RMTg, VTA/SNc-related dopaminergic systems, raphe and other midbrain targets | Bilateral/ipsilateral components | Predominantly glutamatergic LHb projection neurons; often act via inhibitory RMTg intermediates on dopamine systems | Negative reward prediction, aversion and behavioral suppression; theta/low-frequency/gamma interactions depend on state | Established |

## Thalamic association, claustral, and ascending arousal systems

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Pulvinar–cortical projections | Thalamocortical association system | Pulvinar nuclei | Posterior thalamic radiations | Parietal, temporal and occipital association cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral | Thalamic relay neurons; cortical termination spans superficial and middle/deep layers depending subdivision | Visual attention and cortical coordination; alpha/gamma synchrony frequently implicated | Established; subdivision-specific topography |
| Mediodorsal thalamus–prefrontal projections | Thalamocortical association system | Mediodorsal thalamic nucleus | Anterior thalamic peduncle/internal capsule | DLPFC, OFC, ACC and related frontal cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral | Thalamic relay neurons with reciprocal corticothalamic projections | Working memory, decision-making and executive control; theta/beta coordination | Established |
| Intralaminar thalamocortical projections | Diffuse thalamocortical system | Centromedian/parafascicular and related intralaminar nuclei | Diffuse thalamic radiations | Frontal, cingulate, insular and widespread cortical territories; parallel thalamostriatal projections are separate | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral network | Thalamic relay neurons with widespread cortical terminations | Arousal, salience and state control; delta/spindle/high-frequency effects depend on consciousness state | Established |
| Claustrocortical projections | Reciprocal corticoclaustral system | Claustrum | External/extreme capsular region | Widespread frontal, parietal, temporal, sensory and association cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral interactions | Claustral projection neurons, largely excitatory; cortical reciprocal inputs arise from multiple layers | Cross-modal/salience/attention coordination; gamma-synchrony 'binding' interpretations remain debated | Anatomy established; strong unitary 'consciousness hub' claims speculative |
| Claustrothalamic projections | Proposed/subcortical projection system | Claustrum | Deep subcortical pathways | Intralaminar/MD thalamic territories proposed | Uncertain | Claustral projection neurons; human cell-specific evidence limited | Possible salience/state gating; theta-gamma claims are speculative | Probable/uncertain in humans; do not treat as a settled major tract |
| Reticulothalamocortical / ascending arousal system | Distributed ascending state-control network | Pontomesencephalic reticular formation plus monoaminergic/cholinergic/hypothalamic/basal-forebrain systems | Direct and thalamic/basal-forebrain relays | Widespread cortex | Bilateral diffuse | Heterogeneous glutamatergic, cholinergic, noradrenergic, serotonergic, histaminergic, orexinergic and other populations | Wakefulness, attention and sleep–wake transitions; slow oscillations, alpha, spindles and gamma vary by state | Established as a distributed network; not a single reticular→thalamus→cortex tract |

## Diffuse neuromodulatory, hypothalamic, autonomic, and neuroendocrine systems

| Pathway / system | Entity type | Source | Relay(s) / anatomical course | Destination(s) | Crossing / laterality | Cell / layer / transmitter features | Function & physiological correlates | Human evidence / status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Mesolimbic dopaminergic pathway | Neuromodulatory projection system | VTA | Medial forebrain/basal forebrain routes | Nucleus accumbens, amygdala, hippocampal and limbic targets | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral diffuse influence | Dopaminergic neurons, heterogeneous co-transmission by subpopulation | Reward learning, motivation and salience; theta-gamma and phasic dopamine dynamics | Established |
| Mesocortical dopaminergic pathway | Neuromodulatory projection system | VTA | Ascending medial forebrain routes | Prefrontal and cingulate cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral network | Dopaminergic projections to superficial and deep cortical layers; receptor effects cell-type specific | Working memory, cognitive flexibility and motivation; theta/delta/beta modulation varies by task | Established |
| Basal-forebrain cholinergic cortical system | Neuromodulatory projection system | Nucleus basalis of Meynert and related basal-forebrain cholinergic groups | Diffuse basal forebrain → cortical white matter and intracortical arborization | Widespread neocortex and amygdalar/limbic targets by cell group | Bilateral diffuse | Cholinergic neurons with highly branched axons; cortical terminals span multiple layers | Arousal, attention, plasticity and sensory gain; alpha suppression and gamma enhancement at network level | Established; broader than nucleus basalis→neocortex alone |
| Septohippocampal cholinergic/GABAergic pathway | Neuromodulatory projection system | Medial septum and diagonal band | Fornical/fimbrial and related basal-forebrain routes | Hippocampal formation and entorhinal cortex | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral organization | Cholinergic and GABAergic projection neurons | Strong control of hippocampal theta and theta-gamma organization; memory/attention state regulation | Established |
| Noradrenergic locus-coeruleus system | Neuromodulatory projection system | Locus coeruleus | Diffuse ascending and descending projections | Cortex, thalamus, hippocampus, cerebellum, brainstem and spinal targets | Bilateral diffuse | Noradrenergic neurons with extensive axonal arborization | Arousal, vigilance, uncertainty and adaptive gain; broad effects on slow rhythms and gamma rather than a single 'high-gamma tract' signature | Established |
| Serotonergic raphe system | Neuromodulatory projection system | Dorsal/median and other raphe nuclei | Diffuse ascending/descending projections | Cortex, limbic structures, basal ganglia, brainstem and spinal cord | Bilateral diffuse | Serotonergic neurons with receptor-specific excitatory/inhibitory effects | Mood, sleep, behavioral flexibility and pain modulation; delta/theta and state-dependent oscillatory effects | Established |
| Histaminergic tuberomammillary system | Neuromodulatory projection system | Tuberomammillary nucleus | Diffuse ascending/descending hypothalamic projections | Cortex, thalamus, basal forebrain, hypothalamus and brainstem | Bilateral diffuse | Histaminergic neurons | Wakefulness and attention; network activation across multiple frequency bands | Established |
| Orexin/hypocretin projection system | Neuromodulatory projection system | Lateral/perifornical hypothalamic orexin neurons | Diffuse hypothalamic ascending/descending projections | LC, raphe, TMN, basal forebrain, VTA, cortex and other targets | Bilateral diffuse | Orexin-A/B peptidergic neurons, predominantly excitatory/modulatory | Wake stability, motivation and autonomic integration; loss is central to narcolepsy type 1 | Established |
| Medial forebrain bundle (MFB) | Shared longitudinal bundle / heterogeneous projection system | Brainstem, hypothalamic, basal-forebrain and forebrain sources | Longitudinal lateral hypothalamic/basal forebrain corridor | Reciprocal limbic, hypothalamic, basal-forebrain, midbrain and frontal targets | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral components | Contains dopaminergic, noradrenergic, serotonergic, glutamatergic and other fibers depending level/component | Reward, motivation, arousal and autonomic integration | Established but heterogeneous; should not be treated as one neurotransmitter pathway |
| Prefrontal corticohypothalamic projections | Descending limbic/autonomic projection | mPFC/OFC and related medial frontal cortex | Deep forebrain pathways through basal forebrain/hypothalamic approach | PVN, lateral hypothalamus and other hypothalamic nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral/bilateral network | Deep-layer glutamatergic cortical pyramidal neurons | Autonomic, endocrine and stress regulation; slow/theta network coupling | Established at system level |
| Insular→hypothalamic projections (corticohypothalamic / insular-hypothalamic) | Descending interoceptive-autonomic projection | Anterior and mid-insula | Deep forebrain pathways | Hypothalamic nuclei | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral network effects | Deep-layer glutamatergic pyramidal neurons | Visceral-autonomic integration and homeostasis; slow/theta coupling | Established at system level; consolidates two redundant legacy labels |
| Insular→brainstem autonomic projections | Descending interoceptive-autonomic projection | Anterior/mid-insula | Descending through subcortical pathways | Nucleus tractus solitarius, parabrachial complex and related autonomic nuclei | Bilateral/ipsilateral components | Deep-layer cortical projection neurons | Interoceptive/autonomic control; respiratory/cardiac/slow-wave coupling | Established at system level |
| Dorsal longitudinal fasciculus | Longitudinal autonomic pathway | Hypothalamic/periaqueductal and brainstem autonomic regions | Periventricular/periaqueductal longitudinal course through brainstem | Medullary and spinal autonomic centers | Predominantly bilateral | Mixed hypothalamic/brainstem projection neurons | Autonomic and endocrine homeostasis; slow state-related activity | Established |
| Hypothalamospinal pathway | Descending autonomic projection system | Posterior/lateral hypothalamic and related autonomic regions | Descending brainstem tegmental/reticular routes | Intermediolateral cell column and spinal autonomic circuits | Predominantly ipsilateral with bilateral components | Mixed glutamatergic/peptidergic hypothalamic projections | Sympathetic/parasympathetic regulation; interruption can contribute to Horner syndrome | Established |
| Retinohypothalamic tract | Sensory hypothalamic projection | Melanopsin-containing intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells and other retinal ganglion cells | Branches from optic/chiasmal system | Suprachiasmatic nucleus and related hypothalamic targets | Bilateral after chiasmal organization | Glutamatergic/PACAP-containing retinal afferents | Circadian photoentrainment and phase resetting | Established |
| Hypothalamo-neurohypophyseal tract | Neurosecretory axonal system | Magnocellular PVN and supraoptic nucleus neurons | Hypothalamus → infundibulum | Posterior pituitary | Bilateral glandular projection | Oxytocin- and vasopressin-containing magnocellular axons | Water balance, lactation, parturition and neuroendocrine regulation; burst-pattern secretion rather than cortical EEG physiology | Established |
| Tuberoinfundibular neurosecretory system / TIDA | Neurosecretory projection system | Arcuate/periventricular hypothalamic neurons | Median eminence | Hypophyseal portal circulation/anterior pituitary control | Local hypothalamic-pituitary axis | Includes dopaminergic TIDA neurons plus peptide-releasing systems | Prolactin and anterior-pituitary regulation; neuroendocrine pulsatility | Established |

## Shared white-matter conduits and surgical/anatomical landmarks

| Structure | Entity type | Anatomical use / constituent systems |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Corona radiata | Shared conduit | Fan-shaped cortical projection fibers between cortex and internal capsule; contains corticofugal and thalamocortical systems, not a single tract. |
| Internal capsule — anterior limb | Shared conduit | Major frontothalamic/thalamofrontal, frontopontine and related projection fibers. |
| Internal capsule — genu | Shared conduit | Prominent corticobulbar/corticonuclear fibers plus additional thalamocortical/corticothalamic fibers. |
| Internal capsule — posterior limb | Shared conduit | Prominent corticospinal, somatosensory thalamocortical and related projection fibers. |
| Retrolenticular internal capsule | Shared conduit | Posterior thalamic radiations including much of optic-radiation system, plus posterior association/projection fibers. |
| Sublenticular internal capsule | Shared conduit | Auditory radiations and temporopontine/other temporal projection fibers. |
| Cerebral peduncle / crus cerebri | Shared conduit | Major descending corticofugal fibers; topography varies and should not be equated with a single tract. |
| External capsule | Shared conduit | Contains corticocortical and claustrocortical fibers; overlaps route descriptions for IFOF/uncinate and other systems. |
| Extreme capsule | Shared conduit | Association corridor between insula and claustrum; important for ventral language/semantic pathways. |
| Temporal stem | Shared conduit | Compact surgical/anatomical corridor carrying uncinate, IFOF, optic/auditory radiations and other temporal/limbic fibers. |
| Superior cerebellar peduncle | Shared conduit | Major cerebellar efferent route; also carries ventral/rostral spinocerebellar afferents. Many cerebellar efferents decussate in caudal midbrain. |
| Middle cerebellar peduncle | Shared conduit | Pontocerebellar fibers; overwhelmingly afferent to the cerebellum. |
| Inferior cerebellar peduncle | Shared conduit | Carries dorsal spinocerebellar, cuneocerebellar, olivocerebellar, vestibulocerebellar and other fibers; includes restiform/juxtarestiform components. |
| Fields of Forel (H/H1/H2 region) | Subthalamic fiber region / conduit | Contains/converges pallidothalamic and cerebellothalamic fibers; major functional-neurosurgical landmark. |

## Debated/speculative items and evidence cautions

| Item | Classification | Rule for future use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Superior fronto-occipital fasciculus (SFOF) | Debated named fasciculus | Do not treat as an unqualified canonical human tract; studies disagree whether a distinct long bundle exists versus neighboring projection/association fibers. |
| Claustrothalamic 'consciousness gating' | Speculative functional interpretation | Keep any anatomical claustrothalamic projection evidence separate from strong causal claims that it gates consciousness. |
| Single EEG-band labels assigned to long white-matter tracts | Methodological caution | Treat alpha/beta/gamma/theta associations as network/task correlates unless pathway-specific stimulation, lesion, conduction, or invasive evidence supports a tract-specific claim. |
| ALIC/CSTC 'write' interventions | Speculative functional interpretation | Capsulotomy and DBS of anterior-limb CSTC fibers are disruptive interventions; do not infer that adding coupling in the same fibers produces a graded gain in the associated behavior. |
